{:edb/db-before-t 2857 :edb/db-after-t 2858 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536170 50 #inst "2026-10-09T18:04:39.420Z" 13194139536170 true] [17592186128969 1100 "Consider the arc of the curve $y=e^x$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=2$. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis?" 13194139536170 false] [17592186128969 1100 "Consider the arc of the curve $y=e^x$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=2.$ What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis?" 13194139536170 true] [17592186128969 1145 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi y\\,\\textrm d s\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y = f(x)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} y \\sqrt{1+ \\left(\\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\right)^2} \\: \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $x_1=0$ to $x_2={2}$.\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(e^{x}) = e^{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= 2π∫_{x_{1}}^{x_{2}}y\\sqrt{1 + (\\frac{dy}{dx})^{2}}dx \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{2}e^{x}\\sqrt{1 + (e^{x})^{2}}dx \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{2}e^{x}\\sqrt{1 + e^{2x}}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536170 false] [17592186128969 1145 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi y\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y = f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} y \\sqrt{1+ \\left( \\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\right)^2} \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $x_1=0$ to $x_2={2}.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})={e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{x}\\sqrt{1+({e}^{x})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{x}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536170 true] [17592186128970 1100 "Consider the arc of the curve $y=\\sqrt{x-2}$ that lies between the lines $x=2$ and $x=4$. Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis." 13194139536170 false] [17592186128970 1100 "Consider the arc of the curve $y=\\sqrt{x-2}$ that lies between the lines $x=2$ and $x=4.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis." 13194139536170 true] [17592186128970 1145 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi y\\,\\textrm d s\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y = f(x)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} y \\sqrt{1+ \\left(\\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\right)^2} \\: \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $x_1=2$ to $x_2=4$.\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\sqrt{x - 2}) = \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x - 2}}⇒(\\frac{dy}{dx})^{2} = (1)/(4(x - 2))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= 2π∫_{x_{1}}^{x_{2}}y\\sqrt{1 + (\\frac{dy}{dx})^{2}}dx \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{x - 2}\\sqrt{1 + (1)/(4(x - 2))}dx \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{x - 2}\\sqrt{(4x - 7)/(4(x - 2))}dx \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{x - 2}(\\frac{\\sqrt{4x - 7}}{2\\sqrt{x - 2}})dx \\\\\n&= π∫_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{4x - 7}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the substitution\n\n$$\nu=4x-7 \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad \\textrm du = 4\\,\\textrm dx\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= π∫_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{4x - 7}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{4}∫_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{u}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{4} \\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{u^{3}} \\mid _{1}^{9} \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{6}(27 - 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{13π}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536170 false] [17592186128970 1145 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi y\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y = f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} y \\sqrt{1+ \\left( \\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\right)^2} \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $x_1=2$ to $x_2=4.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x-2})=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x-2}}\\,⇒\\,{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4(x-2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{x-2}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4(x-2)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{x-2}\\sqrt{\\frac{4x-7}{4(x-2)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{x-2}(\\frac{\\sqrt{4x-7}}{2\\sqrt{x-2}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{4x-7}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the substitution \n\n$$\nu=4x-7 \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad \\textrm du = 4\\,\\textrm dx,\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{4x-7}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}\\,|}_{1}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}(27-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{13\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536170 true] [17592186128971 1100 "Consider the arc of the curve $x=y^3-y$ that lies in the second quadrant between its $y$-intercepts. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/assets/1bdbe21cbb517645dfe9362f3313c2d85118acfea1d839fe33ae9b9ac6c3ed50.png)" 13194139536170 true] [17592186128971 1100 "Consider the arc of the curve $x=y^3-y$ that lies in the second quadrant between its $y$-intercepts. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1994/Source/Images/2e7b78757d93bacc9371bde4d7088279.png)" 13194139536170 false] [17592186128971 1145 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi y\\,\\textrm d s\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x = f(y)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d y}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d y\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{y_1}^{y_2} y \\sqrt{1+ \\left(\\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} \\right)^2} \\: \\textrm{d}y\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{3} - y &= 0 \\\\\ny(y^{2} - 1) &= 0 \\\\\ny &= 0, ± 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=1$.\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dy} &= \\frac{d}{dy}(y^{3} - y) = 3y^{2} - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= 2π∫_{y_{1}}^{y_{2}}y\\sqrt{1 + (\\frac{dx}{dy})^{2}}dy \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{1}y\\sqrt{1 + (3y^{2} - 1)^{2}}dy \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{1}y\\sqrt{2 - 6y^{2} + 9y^{4}}dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536170 false] [17592186128971 1145 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi y\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x = f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d y}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{y_1}^{y_2} y \\sqrt{1+ \\left( \\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} \\right)^2} \\: \\textrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{3}-y & =0 \\\\ y({y}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{3}-y)=3{y}^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\sqrt{1+{(3{y}^{2}-1)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\sqrt{2-6{y}^{2}+9{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536170 true] [17592186129150 1020 #edb/ref 17592186387028 13194139536170 true] [17592186129150 1020 #edb/ref 17592186387035 13194139536170 true] [17592186129150 1020 #edb/ref 17592186387042 13194139536170 true] [17592186129150 1020 #edb/ref 17592186387049 13194139536170 true] [17592186129150 1020 #edb/ref 17592186387077 13194139536170 true] [17592186129151 1020 #edb/ref 17592186387070 13194139536170 true] [17592186129151 1020 #edb/ref 17592186387091 13194139536170 true] [17592186129151 1020 #edb/ref 17592186387098 13194139536170 true] [17592186129151 1020 #edb/ref 17592186387101 13194139536170 true] [17592186129151 1020 #edb/ref 17592186387104 13194139536170 true] [17592186129152 1020 #edb/ref 17592186387056 13194139536170 true] [17592186129152 1020 #edb/ref 17592186387063 13194139536170 true] [17592186129152 1020 #edb/ref 17592186387084 13194139536170 true] [17592186129347 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536170 true] [17592186129347 1100 "Consider the arc of the curve $x = \\tan y$ that lies between $y = 0$ and $y = \\frac{π}{4}$. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2π$ radians about the $x$-axis?" 13194139536170 false] [17592186129347 1100 "Consider the arc of the curve $x=\\operatorname{tan}⁡y$ that lies between $y=0$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" 13194139536170 true] [17592186129347 1145 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${y}_{1}=0$ to ${y}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡y)={sec}^{2}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}y\\sqrt{1+{({sec}^{2}⁡y)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}y\\sqrt{1+{sec}^{4}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536170 true] [17592186129349 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536170 true] [17592186129349 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/assets/dc94ee46e2b9381afb6f62b74c67d76d137f857c3f78f36df2fdad5661b114b3.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x=2y-{y}^{3}$ that lies in the first quadrant between its $y$-intercepts. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" 13194139536170 true] [17592186129349 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1994/Source/Images/q-109179.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x = 2y - y^{3}$ that lies in the first quadrant between its $y$-intercepts. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2π$ radians about the $x$-axis?" 13194139536170 false] [17592186129349 1145 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y-{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ y(2-{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=\\sqrt{2}.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2y-{y}^{3})=2-3{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(2-3{y}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}y\\sqrt{9{y}^{4}-12{y}^{2}+5}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387028 1017 #edb/ref 17592186387029 13194139536170 true] [17592186387028 1089 "q-109123" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387028 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536170 true] [17592186387028 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/assets/924444fa2da1709d788d3b3522e86743b46ee1c5a25999bb77731449e621a07d.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $y=1-(x+1)^{2}$ that lies in the second quadrant between its $x$-intercepts. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387028 1113 #uuid "caf996b3-98c2-50cc-98cb-eb0ffa63955b" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387028 1145 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-(x+1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ x+1 & =\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =-1\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =-2,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-2$ to $x=0.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1-(x+1)^{2})=-2x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}(1-(x+1)^{2})\\sqrt{1+(-2x-2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}(-{x}^{2}-2x)\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+8x+5}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387029 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387030 13194139536170 true] [17592186387029 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387031 13194139536170 true] [17592186387029 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387032 13194139536170 true] [17592186387029 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387033 13194139536170 true] [17592186387029 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387034 13194139536170 true] [17592186387029 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536170 true] [17592186387029 1041 "selection" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387029 1104 #edb/ref 17592186387030 13194139536170 true] [17592186387029 1110 #uuid "f2b847f4-9c39-5384-8695-2e0e2e99e7bf" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387030 1034 "2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}(-{x}^{2}-2x)\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+8x+5}\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387030 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536170 true] [17592186387030 1111 #uuid "7a26fcb7-dd8a-5338-afc8-d3346caf5af3" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387031 1034 "4\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}(-{x}^{2}-2x)\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+5x}\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387031 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536170 true] [17592186387031 1111 #uuid "a141414b-dd92-556c-8029-8f7e488e8a14" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387032 1034 "-2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}(x+1)^{2}\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+8x+5}\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387032 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536170 true] [17592186387032 1111 #uuid "73c71bf5-78f3-503c-a5c2-9b1777e83a99" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387033 1034 "4\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}(-x-1)\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+5x}\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387033 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536170 true] [17592186387033 1111 #uuid "6d2363e5-fed2-505d-a7b3-cfd6f73c84f6" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387034 1034 "-2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+8x+5}\\,\\text{