ue] [17592186387042 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536170 true] [17592186387042 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/assets/0edb5218c2d1527957053a35e992c7538a8857132a6d48114e16e5f71d3848d6.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $y=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}$ that lies in the first quadrant between the line $x=0$ and its $x$-intercept. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387042 1113 #uuid "d3725a9b-dcb4-5fa2-b792-1fe048d5722b" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387042 1145 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 4-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}})\\sqrt{1+{(-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_