s/multivariable/14082610/assets/22bb16a874005f09d5cbad055f149f07e2139f559a2a04368c32696567714b26.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x=\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}$ that lies in the first quadrant between the line $y=0$ and its $y$-intercept. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387056 1113 #uuid "52c76f08-0943-5c3e-9636-b2f7eab362d4" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387056 1145 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =2 \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=\\sqrt{2}.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}y\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}y\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{2-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387057 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387058 13194139536170 true] [17592186387057 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387059 13194139536170 true] [17592186387057 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387060 13194139536170 true] [17592186387057 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387061 13194139536170 true] [17592186387057 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387062 13194139536170 true] [17592186387057 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536170 true] [17592186387057 1041 "selection" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387057 1104 #edb/ref 17592186387059 13194139536170 true] [17592186387057 1110 #uuid "d9b2d52e-608f-56ca-b7c9-86ab9b88ad0e" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387058 1034 "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387058 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536170 true] [17592186387058 1111 #uuid "c3846b18-068f-5d46-b8e9-ae330a812c0f" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387059 1034 "2\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387059 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536170 true] [17592186387059 1111 #uuid "ff5ee6ab-b700-5c7a-8309-f7bc7b0e046a" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387060 1034 "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387060 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536170 true] [17592186387060 1111 #uuid "cd0482db-620c-5115-9d37-2e8fd660b699" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387061 1034 "2\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387061 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536170 true] [17592186387061 1111 #uuid "2f29ee2e-c0e3-59ac-9766-f0fd265878bb" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387062 1034 "\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}}{y}\\,\\text{d}y" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387062 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536170 true] [17592186387062 1111 #uuid "f86696a7-7152-54f4-b6f6-64a05f3e2976" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387063 1017 #edb/ref 17592186387064 13194139536170 true] [17592186387063 1089 "q-109188" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387063 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536170 true] [17592186387063 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/assets/9b27f44aa7ef6bef95a88ccfcb6495d17e9457a7452bf602f87dcd5b95ec71dc.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ that lies in the first quadrant between its $y$-intercept and the line $y=e.$ What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387063 1113 #uuid "6cb738bd-1657-50aa-acee-1f912676f25f" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387063 1145 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y} & ={e}^{0} \\\\ y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=1$ to $y=e.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=\\frac{1}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{1}{y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}y\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387064 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387065 13194139536170 true] [17592186387064 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387066 13194139536170 true] [17592186387064 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387067 13194139536170 true] [17592186387064 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387068 13194139536170 true] [17592186387064 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387069 13194139536170 true] [17592186387064 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536170 true] [17592186387064 1041 "selection" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387064 1104 #edb/ref 17592186387068 13194139536170 true] [17592186387064 1110 #uuid "93548155-9136-514c-9ff4-2ad626f0b8d7" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387065 1034 "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}y\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}y" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387065 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536170 true] [17592186387065 1111 #uuid "922c0fe5-a954-57dd-9c66-c82340b63378" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387066 1034 "2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{e}\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}y" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387066 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536170 true] [17592186387066 1111 #uuid "b8214ee2-7c44-59c9-b036-88a88600709a" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387067 1034 "2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{e}y\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}y" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387067 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536170 true] [17592186387067 1111 #uuid "d94a6cfb-ed38-5eb5-9db9-c191cb50a173" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387068 1034 "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}y" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387068 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536170 true] [17592186387068 1111 #uuid "60cc550d-4716-5c87-8ade-1c3b3b9567f4" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387069 1034 "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\sqrt{y({y}^{2}+1)}\\,\\text{d}y" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387069 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536170 true] [17592186387069 1111 #uuid "9d8c08f0-c24d-5981-89cf-b253b8083cb5" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387070 1017 #edb/ref 17592186387071 13194139536170 true] [17592186387070 1089 "q-109319" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387070 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139536170 true] [17592186387070 1100 "Consider the arc of the curve $x=\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}$ that lies between the lines $y=0$ and $y=2.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." 13194139536170 true] [17592186387070 1113 #uuid "777aa170-0dc9-512e-8f03-a8a705e1c469" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387070 1145 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${y}_{1}=0$ to ${y}_{2}=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}}\\,⇒\\,{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9-{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{9-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\frac{3y}{\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =3\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{2y}{\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the substitution \n\n$$\nu=9-{y}^{2}\\,⇒\\,\\text{d}u=-2y\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =3\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{2y}{\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-3\\pi {\\int }_{9}^{5}{u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\pi {\\int }_{5}^{9}{u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\pi {[2\\sqrt{u}\\,]}_{5}^{9} \\\\  & =6\\pi (3-\\sqrt{5}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387071 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387072 13194139536170 true] [17592186387071 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387073 13194139536170 true] [17592186387071 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387074 13194139536170 true] [17592186387071 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387075 13194139536170 true] [17592186387071 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387076 13194139536170 true] [17592186387071 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536170 true] [17592186387071 1041 "selection" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387071 1104 #edb/ref 17592186387072 13194139536170 true] [17592186387071 1110 #uuid "4306813d-1d6e-5b41-aec2-235f501e995a" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387072 1034 "6\\pi (3-\\sqrt{5})" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387072 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536170 true] [17592186387072 1111 #uuid "a227ddfa-8f31-529a-aced-ec8333463edf" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387073 1034 "\\frac{\\pi }{2}(5-\\sqrt{3})" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387073 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536170 true] [17592186387073 1111 #uuid "3f407bcc-8ed3-5d07-8aa0-85b60cd8bcc3" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387074 1034 "2\\pi (6-\\sqrt{5})" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387074 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536170 true] [17592186387074 1111 #uuid "3f79674a-5183-5d7b-9737-fbfcf90030ed" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387075 1034 "\\frac{\\pi }{4}(2-\\sqrt{5})" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387075 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536170 true] [17592186387075 1111 #uuid "3bf3ffbc-d7e5-582c-9ce3-204ddbd465f1" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387076 1034 "4\\pi (2-\\sqrt{3})" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387076 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536170 true] [17592186387076 1111 #uuid "73d6c78a-807e-566c-8a7b-820218a705df" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387077 1017 #edb/ref 17592186387078 13194139536170 true] [17592186387077 1089 "q-126521" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387077 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536170 true] [17592186387077 1100 "Consider the arc of the curve $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387077 1113 #uuid "c8299664-2eac-54b1-a2d8-b4c54e28a46f" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387077 1145 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\sqrt{1+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387078 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387079 13194139536170 true] [17592186387078 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387080 13194139536170 true] [17592186387078 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387081 13194139536170 true] [17592186387078 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387082 13194139536170 true] [17592186387078 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387083 13194139536170 true] [17592186387078 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536170 true] [17592186387078 1041 "selection" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387078 1104 #edb/ref 17592186387081 1319