tercepts. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387084 1113 #uuid "01d978c2-8b03-5808-9b4a-ab2bf7c73b35" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387084 1145 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-2y & =0 \\\\ y(y-2) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2}-2y)=2y-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{1+{(2y-2)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}-8y+5}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387085 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387086 13194139536170 true] [17592186387085 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387087 13194139536170 true] [17592186387085 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387088 13194139536170 true] [17592186387085 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387089 13194139536170 true] [17592186387085 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387090 13194139536170 true] [17592186387085 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536170 true] [17592186387085 1041 "selection" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387085 1104 #edb/ref 17592186387086 13194139536170 true] [17592186387085 1110 #uuid "2cb6d9f6-2140-5991-b864-4fea6b588ca1" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387086 1034 "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}-8y+5}\\,\\text{d}y" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387086 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536170 true] [17592186387086 1111 #uuid "0c157c29-07e2-5505-aefe-3127849fc32f" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387087 1034 "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}+5}\\,\\text{d}y" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387087 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536170 true] [17592186387087 1111 #uuid "c14f66cb-598d-53b3-a7d9-7a82afc56072" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387088 1034 "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}-8y+1}\\,\\text{d}y" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387088 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536170 true] [17592186387088 1111 #uuid "f48ceb38-e9d5-5645-9034-4ddd4d3c1763" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387089 1034 "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({y}^{2}-2y)\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}+5}\\,\\text{d}y" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387089 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536170 true] [17592186387089 1111 #uuid "bdffeeda-8453-5f19-94c8-d471dbddd8ae" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387090 1034 "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2}-2y)\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}-8y+5}\\,\\text{d}y" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387090 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536170 true] [17592186387090 1111 #uuid "3aa65cf1-8862-5e81-9eb0-646b769e8f3e" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387091 1017 #edb/ref 17592186387092 13194139536170 true] [17592186387091 1089 "q-126589" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387091 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139536170 true] [17592186387091 1100 "Consider the arc of the curve $x=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-1$ that lies between the lines $y=0$ and $y=1.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." 13194139536170 true] [17592186387091 1113 #uuid "89554c02-d315-5a8d-8464-ca9cd53eae70" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387091 1145 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${y}_{1}=0$ to ${y}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-1)=y\\,⇒\\,{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the substitution \n\n$$\nu={y}^{2}+1\\,⇒\\,(\\frac{1}{2})\\text{d}u=y\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{u}\\,(\\frac{1}{2})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\pi {\\frac{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}{3}\\,|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}(\\sqrt{{2}^{3}}-\\sqrt{{1}^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}(2\\sqrt{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387092 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387093 13194139536170 true] [17592186387092 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387094 13194139536170 true] [17592186387092 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387095 13194139536170 true] [17592186387092 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387096 13194139536170 true] [17592186387092 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387097 13194139536170 true] [17592186387092 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536170 true] [17592186387092 1041 "selection" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387092 1104 #edb/ref 17592186387094 13194139536170 true] [17592186387092 1110 #uuid "09769ad5-a9f0-5417-8bf1-c063e61e33a9" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387093 1034 "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}\\,\\pi }{2}" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387093 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536170 true] [17592186387093 1111 #uuid "0c828721-be3c-5849-8600-f8b2e58afe67" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387094 1034 "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}(2\\sqrt{2}-1)" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387094 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536170 true] [17592186387094 1111 #uuid "dc98e8cd-9d57-5de2-89b9-78853d7a23cf" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387095 1034 "2\\pi (\\sqrt{2}-1)" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387095 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536170 true] [17592186387095 1111 #uuid "9fe596ad-226e-55fa-a4f4-39c8f3e379f6" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387096 1034 "\\pi (3\\sqrt{2}-1)" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387096 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536170 true] [17592186387096 1111 #uuid "a5d3ed15-fc6a-558c-b1f1-13345a642e17" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387097 1034 "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\,\\pi }{3}" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387097 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536170 true] [17592186387097 1111 #uuid "c4028995-1484-5909-9581-c2d66f900e96" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387098 1017 #edb/ref 17592186387099 13194139536170 true] [17592186387098 1089 "q-353403" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387098 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536170 true] [17592186387098 1100 "Consider the arc of the curve $y=\\sqrt{3x+1}$ that lies between the lines $x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ and $x=1.$ Find the surface area $S$ generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$ \n{{field-1}}" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387098 1113 #uuid "a2419aff-382b-524d-8eee-9f4042d5d08e" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387098 1145 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3}$ to ${x}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{3x+1})=\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3x+1}}\\,⇒\\,{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}=\\frac{9}{4(3x+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1/3}^{1}\\sqrt{3x+1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{9}{4(3x+1)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1/3}^{1}\\sqrt{3x+1}\\sqrt{\\frac{12x+13}{4(3x+1)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1/3}^{1}\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{12x+13}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/3}^{1}\\sqrt{12x+13}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot \\frac{(12x+13)^{3/2}}{18}\\,{|}_{-1/3}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{18}({25}^{3/2}-{9}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{49\\pi }{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387099 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387100 13194139536170 true] [17592186387099 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139536170 true] [17592186387099 1041 "field-1" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387099 1104 #edb/ref 17592186387100 13194139536170 true] [17592186387099 1110 #uuid "83bd1d8e-494a-50ce-a109-f8a42a88b9d8" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387100 1034 "\\frac{49\\pi}{9}" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387100 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536170 true] [17592186387100 1111 #uuid "753d94e0-a2a2-549f-8dc0-d6b142b11e2c" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387101 1017 #edb/ref 17592186387102 13194139536170 true] [17592186387101 1089 "q-353405" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387101 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536170 true] [17592186387101 1100 "Consider the arc of the curve $y=2x+1$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=1.$ Find the surface area $S$ generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$ \n{{field-1}}" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387101 1113 #uuid "45434f1e-aeeb-591d-8147-87710b9c595b" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387101 1145 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${x}_{1}=0$ to ${x}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x+1)=2\\,⇒\\,{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+1)\\sqrt{1+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+1)\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi \\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi \\sqrt{5}({x}^{2}+x){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\pi \\sqrt{5}((1+1)-0) \\\\  & =4\\pi \\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387102 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387103 13194139536170 true] [17592186387102 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139536170 true] [17592186387102 1041 "field-1" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387102 1104 #edb/ref 17592186387103 13194139536170 true] [17592186387102 1110 #uuid "a7036810-ada4-5374-a85e-4c88fa0fa505" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387103 1034 "4\\pi\\sqrt{5}" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387103 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536170 true] [17592186387103 1111 #uuid "89fec85d-f8a0-5718-825e-7f130d1db9e5" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387104 1017 #edb/ref 17592186387105 13194139536170 true] [17592186387104 1089 "q-353411" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387104 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139536170 true] [17592186387104 1100 "Consider the arc of the curve $x=\\frac{4}{3}{y}^{3/2}$ that lies between the lines $y=0$ and $y=2.$ Calculate the surface area $S$ generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387104 1113 #uuid "83dd7e58-e9f5-5d83-b520-e7682449df98" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387104 1145 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${y}_{1}=0$ to ${y}_{2}=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{4}{3}{y}^{3/2})=2\\sqrt{y}\\,⇒\\,{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}=4y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{1+4y}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the substitution \n\n$$\nu=1+4y\\,⇒\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}y,\\,y=\\frac{u-1}{4},\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{1+4y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{9}\\frac{u-1}{4}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}{\\int }_{1}^{9}(u-1)\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}{\\int }_{1}^{9}({u}^{3/2}-{u}^{1/2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}(\\frac{2}{5}{u}^{5/2}-\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2})\\,{|}_{1}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}((\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {9}^{5/2}-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {9}^{3/2})-(\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {1}^{5/2}-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {1}^{3/2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}((\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot 243-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 27)-(\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{2}{3})) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}(\\frac{396}{5}+\\frac{4}{15}) \\\\  & =\\frac{149\\pi }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387105 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387106 13194139536170 true] [17592186387105 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139536170 true] [17592186387105 1041 "field-1" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387105 1104 #edb/ref 17592186387106 13194139536170 true] [17592186387105 1110 #uuid "1a1e728d-36c5-5ec0-987c-1723f9717512" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387106 1034 "\\frac{149\\pi}{15}" 13194139536170 true] [17592186387106 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536170 true] [17592186387106 1111 #uuid "7bd5631d-1ba6-5ff0-913d-bd4ab5adf0f7" 13194139536170 true]] :edb/tempids {"q-109123" 17592186387028 "q-109123/selection" 17592186387029 "q-109123/selection/41a589383802c8d783a9a98b2382e8bdb1d828a7227e9474750e9ae08f1a0bf9" 17592186387030 "q-109123/selection/8b4825c9596ea242c96ada9b7ceb1e4346e56c0c28fc908f46567438744dd569" 17592186387031 "q-109123/selection/d3af307f96a18f5815958f66d52077b5520f36ec01179bf447375054da37040c" 17592186387032 "q-109123/selection/efd0be725da523cb6bbac1f73df3e4b73d15bef51c4f6cbc7ae5370017e6dbfd" 17592186387033 "q-109123/selection/fbe98b6a0949b2fb8e2db9c2ecb0fcb79bde35a715e8ca022e91c7453bd4a68d" 17592186387034 "q-109124" 17592186387035 "q-109124/selection" 17592186387036 "q-109124/selection/32d7d19f6ae355a1838dd13c6d7865d8f9254defca677a696ff1b5ab39ce76fe" 17592186387037 "q-109124/selection/4dab9eea3ad3a3a8915168799fab063d3a7cba322f1aa7974db1c28792a931bc" 17592186387038 "q-109124/selection/6c302304d4a35732abf3a37f4b6301ca2e9413cb08cb3701c64c4ae19fd296f4" 17592186387039 "q-109124/selection/b2a311e627e3b0302988dd61f3386a908e13643c935b66edd953e212a9583c4f" 175921